  <static seed="193" source="Library/Michigan/Chap5Sec2/Q27.pg">
    <statement><p>Use the following figure, which shows a graph of <m>f(x)</m> to find each of
the indicated integrals.</p>
<image xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org/2020/pretext/internal" pi:generated="webwork/images/Library__Michigan__Chap5Sec2__Q27_pg-image-1.png" width="33%"/>
<p><em>(Click on the graph for a larger version.)</em></p>
<p>Note that the first area (with vertical, red shading) is 55 and the
second (with oblique, black shading) is 5.</p>
<p><alert>A.</alert>
<m>\int_a^b f(x) dx =</m> <fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p><alert>B.</alert>
<m>\int_b^c f(x) dx =</m> <fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
<p><alert>C.</alert>
<m>\int_a^c f(x) dx =</m> <fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0003"/></p>
<p><alert>D.</alert>
<m>\int_a^c |f(x)| dx =</m> <fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0004"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>55</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>-5</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>50</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>60</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>y = f(x)</m> and the <m>x</m>-axis, with area below the axis being
counted as negative area.  Therefore



<m>\int_a^b f(x) dx = 55</m>,
<m>\int_b^c f(x) dx = -5</m>,
<m>\int_a^c f(x) dx = 55  - 5 = 50</m>, and
<m>\int_a^c |f(x)| dx = |55| + |-5| = 60</m>.



</solution>
